{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0151TUVJkCAkL1KUeAnWm4m3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed predicted data for each \"planned\" experiment in the experiment_plan, under the \"median\" tier definition. Let me identify all experiments with status \"planned\":\n\n1. plan_010 - DCOL component ablation - status: planned, but notes say \"取消\" (cancelled) because plan_004 failed\n2. plan_014 - 13B clean FP16 scale validation (Qwen2.5-14B) - status: RUNNING\n3. plan_015 - Wild-data calibration: C4/FineWeb/RedPajama - status: planned\n\n\n4. plan_018 - Existing detector ordinal baseline: Binoculars + Fast-DetectGPT - status: planned\n5. plan_020 - Table 1 bootstrap CIs - status: planned\n6. plan_023 - Theoretical analysis - status: planned\n7. plan_026 - API model recursive chains - status: planned\n\nI should also check if the theory_section what_if needs data. Looking at the request again, it's specifically for \"planned\" experiments, so I need to exclude RUNNING ones like plan_014 and skip the cancelled plan_010. That leaves me with plan_015, plan_018, plan_020, plan_023, and plan_026 to generate predicted data for. Looking at the planned experiments that need detailed predictions, I'm focusing on plan_010 through plan_026. Plan_014 is already running so I'll note what predictions it needs. Now I'm thinking through what the median tier would predict for each of these based on the criteria: 14B signal exists but attenuated with 4-class accuracy around 62-74%, d1v2 AUC between 0.70-0.81, and partial feature ranking shifts. The theory section would provide intuitive explanation rather than rigorous justification, and I'd expect at least one reviewer to question the methodological novelty, putting the projected reviewer average around 3.0-3.5.\n\nStarting with plan_010's DCOL component ablation... Cancelled because plan_004 (DCOL v6) failed. Skip or note as cancelled.\n\n### plan_014 - 13B clean FP16 scale validation (Qwen2.5-14B)\nStatus: RUNNING. This is the key 14B experiment.\nMedian prediction:\n- 4-class acc: 62-74% → let's say 68%\n- d1v2 AUC: 0.70-0.81 → let's say 0.76\n- Feature top-4 overlap with 1B: 2-3 → let's say 2 out of 4\n- d0v1 AUC still high (~0.95+)\n- d2v3 AUC: ~0.65-0.72\n\nAt 14B scale, the model produces higher-quality recursive generations, so perplexity distributions between depths converge and make the depth signal harder to detect. The depth effect persists above chance but weakens because LoRA fine-tuning causes less distribution shift per generation. Feature rankings shift as different text quality aspects become more prominent at larger model capacity.\n\n### plan_015 - Wild-data calibration: C4/FineWeb/RedPajama\nApplying trained RF classifiers to wild data to see how domain shift affects performance. I expect elevated false positive rates and depth distributions skewed toward d0, with substantial shifts in feature distributions. I notice there are already wild_data experiments completed (wild_data_calibration_001 through 005, wild_data_features_001, etc.), so I need to check what's already been done before proceeding.\n\nMedian predictions for this plan:\n- C4 FPR: ~20-35%\n- FineWeb FPR: ~15-25%\n- RedPajama FPR: ~15-30%\n- Depth signal exists but domain shift introduces noise\n- GPU reference model inference may add calibration issues\n\n### plan_018 - Existing detector ordinal baseline\nComparing Binoculars and Fast-DetectGPT scores calibrated for ordinal depth classification. Based on the tier definition, I'm expecting Binoculars and Fast-DetectGPT to achieve 45-60% accuracy on the 4-class ordinal task, with some configurations approaching our weaker cell performance (topk50), which would somewhat diminish the uniqueness of our approach.\n\nFor the discrimination between classes, detectors should perform well on d0v1 (~0.95+) since they're designed for binary detection, but d1v2 and d2v3 comparisons will be much weaker.\n\n### plan_020 - Table 1 bootstrap CIs\nRunning bootstrap confidence intervals on Table 1 results. The CIs should be tight enough to support our claims, though some cells likely have overlapping intervals with adjacent methods.\n\n### plan_023 - Theoretical analysis\nThe theoretical section provides intuitive connections rather than rigorous proofs. The tail erosion link to p90_ppl references Shumailov qualitatively without formal derivation, and the EDD upper bound relies on strong i.i.d. assumptions that reviewers would likely critique as descriptive rather than rigorous.\n\n### plan_026 - API model recursive chains\nAPI models probably show weaker discrimination signals due to RLHF and instruction tuning effects, similar to what we saw with the instruct model baseline.\n\nNow I'm compiling the full YAML output and need to reconcile the theory section with the what_if analysis. For plan_014 with the 14B model, I'm looking at the median tier predictions: 14b_4class_acc around 68, 14b_d1v2_auc around 0.76, and working through the remaining metrics.\n\nFor plan_015 with wild data, the new GPU reference inference across different datasets shows elevated but consistent FPR, with partial transfer of the depth signal. Plan_018's detector baselines are concerning because existing ordinal detectors already achieve 45-60% accuracy on the 4-class task, which somewhat undermines our contribution. I'm also noticing a discrepancy in plan_020 where the bootstrap CI computation appears marked as done in the registry but the plan itself is still listed as planned—I need to clarify this inconsistency.\n\nPlan_023 is purely a writing task that provides motivation but lacks rigor as a contribution. Plan_026 with API models shows weak signals similar to what we see with instruct models. I should also note that plan_010 is cancelled despite being listed as planned, and there's overlap between plan_023 and the theory section in the what_if experiments that I'll need to account for.\n\nNow I'm constructing the YAML output with the median tier narrative and imagined experiments.\n\nFor plan_014 with the 14B Qwen model, I'm looking at how the 1B baselines perform—Pythia nucleus hits 78.2% with d1v2 of 0.861, OLMo nucleus reaches 78.8% with 0.867, and GPT-2 XL nucleus gets 83.3% with 0.913. Given the median tier expectations of 62-74% 4-class accuracy and 0.70-0.81 d1v2 AUC, I'm predicting where the 14B model should land based on these scaling patterns.\n\nAt 14B scale, the model has more capacity to fit training data without as much distributional shift from LoRA fine-tuning, so the perplexity tail statistics should shift less dramatically even as depth increases. The richer internal representations at this scale mean some features like mean_ppl might lose discriminative power while repetition and entropy metrics could become more important—I'd expect maybe 2-3 features to overlap in the top-4 rankings compared to the 1B results.\n\nI'm also tracking the OLMo seed43 case that the median tier flagged, though the current metrics for that run appear incomplete.\n\nNow I need to be systematic about which experiments are actually running versus planned. Plan 014 with the 13B clean FP16 is currently executing, and I should include that alongside the other planned experiments like the wild-data calibration, detector baselines, and bootstrap confidence intervals.\n\nFor the narrative guidance under the median tier, I'm clarifying how the weaker 14B signal forces me to reframe the main claims—shifting from claiming robustness across scales to positioning it as feasible at 1B with preliminary results at 14B. The paper's strength becomes the systematic coverage of methods rather than peak accuracy on any single configuration, with Atlas and EDD remaining the core contributions while the theoretical section serves more as motivation.\n\nThe cross-strategy pipeline has a real bottleneck in the strategy classifier that I need to acknowledge honestly, and realistically the paper's path to acceptance depends on finding a reviewer who values the problem formalization itself.\n\nNow I'm setting up the 14B validation experiment with Qwen2.5-14B, running across the dataset splits to see where performance lands—expecting the random forest to reach around 68% accuracy while the logistic regression drops to 64%, with the real versus synthetic distinction remaining easy to detect but the harder distinctions between synthetic strategies showing more degradation in the 70-80% range.\n\nThe larger model capacity means smaller distribution shifts from LoRA fine-tuning at each generation step, so while perplexity metrics stay in the top features, their discriminative power drops by 15-20 percentage points and the boundaries between adjacent depths blur significantly as the generated text becomes more consistent and higher quality.\n\nNow I'm planning to calibrate against three wild datasets—C4, FineWeb, and RedPajama—using a GPU reference model to properly extract features across all of them. I expect detection rates around 66-85% for depth-0 samples depending on the dataset and reference model, with false positive rates in the 15-34% range, though consistency across different reference models will be moderate. The issue is that web-crawled data contains a lot of templated and SEO content whose statistical properties—high repetition, low diversity—overlap with what recursive generation produces, so most false positives come from low-quality web content rather than genuine synthetic text.\n\nFor the detector baseline, I'm testing Binoculars and Fast-DetectGPT calibrated for ordinal depth classification, expecting around 45-60% accuracy on 4-class tasks. Binoculars should excel at binary detection with an AUC near 0.96, but performance drops significantly for distinguishing between intermediate depth levels, while Fast-DetectGPT achieves slightly better 4-class accuracy overall.\n\nThe core issue is that these detectors were designed to capture the binary real-versus-synthetic signal in their continuous scores, so when calibrated into ordinal bins, they maintain strong d0 detection but struggle to differentiate between d1, d2, and d3 since they lack the architectural design to capture ordinal depth gradients—this limitation becomes especially apparent in weaker cells.\n\nNow I'm moving to bootstrap confidence intervals across all nine cells to quantify uncertainty, expecting widths around 1.5-3.0 percentage points with some adjacent cells showing overlapping intervals, though the best-performing cell (GPT-2 XL at temp09) should maintain clear separation from chance while even the worst cell (Pythia topk50) stays meaningfully above baseline.\n\nFor the theoretical analysis piece, I'm planning to connect tail erosion patterns to the perplexity dominance we're seeing, derive an upper bound for edit distance decay under the assumption of i.i.d. generation (though this breaks down in our LoRA recursive setup), and provide a descriptive account of why temperature scaling converges uniformly while nucleus and topk sampling don't. 为什么p90_ppl和var_surprisal是universal depth markers的直觉解释——Shumailov等人的tail erosion理论预测recursive generation会逐渐消除分布的尾部，这正是p90_ppl捕捉的信号。EDD上界推导在ideal case下是elegant的，但实际LoRA recursive chain违反了i.i.d.假设，使推导的practical applicability受限。strategy-dependent convergence rate的解释（temperature uniform scaling加速收敛）提供了观察到的temp09 d1v2反转现象的理论框架，但这些都是定性的motivation而非独立的理论贡献。\n\n对于API模型的recursive chains，我预期GPT-4o-mini fine-tuned chain不太可行，而prompted recursive chain会产生较弱的信号——4class_acc大约40-50%，d0v1_auc在0.70-0.80之间，d1v2_auc在0.55-0.65之间，整体弱于base models因为RLHF和instruction tuning的影响。\n\nAPI模型受到更强的alignment约束，通过API的recursive fine-tune可能无法产生足够的分布偏移来创建可区分的depth gradient，而prompted recursive chain由于每次rewrite不等同于LoRA fine-tune的分布偏移，信号会更弱。\n\n关于theory section，这与plan_023有大量重叠，只能算是定性的motivation而非独立贡献，应该归入review类别。\n\n现在看OLMo seed43的情况，虽然olmo_seed43_4class_acc的结果在[74, 78]范围内，但olmo_seed43_0